.Im MicroEmacs command|region
.so me.tni
.TH accents-compose-region 2
.XI accents-compose-region - "Compose the accents in the region (Unicode NFC)"
.XI accents-decompose-region - "Decompose the accents in the region (Unicode NFD)"
.SH NAME
.Me "|accents-compose-region"
accents-compose-region \- Compose the accents in the region (Unicode NFC)
.Me $a
.br
.Me "|accents-decompose-region"
accents-decompose-region \- Decompose the accents in the region (Unicode NFD)
.Me $a
.SH SYNOPSIS
.na
\fBaccents-compose-region\fR
.br
\fBaccents-decompose-region\fR
.ad
.SH DESCRIPTION
An accented letter can be held in a file as a single character, e.g. an \fCe\fR with an acute accent (U+00E9), or as
the letter followed by a combining mark, an \fCe\fR (U+0065) and a combining acute accent (U+0301). The two forms look
the same in most programs, but they are different text: one does not find the other in a search, they sort, compare
and spell check differently, and a file name in one form does not match the other. \*(mS shows what is there, drawing a
combining mark as a character of its own (an \fCe\fR followed by an acute accent), see
.Hl "Locale Support" localeSupport 2 .
.PP
.B accents-compose-region
changes the text in the region to the single character form wherever there is one, Unicode's Normalization Form C
(NFC), the form most files, keyboards and web pages use.
.B accents-decompose-region
changes it to the letter and combining mark form, Normalization Form D (NFD), the form macOS file names use.
.PP
Both put a letter's combining marks in Unicode's standard order and change only the text which differs, so the rest of
the region, and any mark in it, is left as it was. They also change Korean letters to syllables (NFC) or syllables to
letters (NFD).
.PP
Both commands can also replace a single character with a different single character, which can look like a fault in a
command that composes accents. Unicode defines some characters as \fIcanonically equivalent\fR to another single
character, the two being the same text which happens to have two codes. For each such pair Unicode names one code as
the standard (\fIcanonical\fR) form, and NFC and NFD both replace the other code with it. The other code is never
produced by either command, so an existing one is changed even though it is already a single character with no
combining mark. Examples are:
.IP \(bu
The Greek letters with an \fIoxia\fR accent in the Greek Extended block, e.g. upsilon with oxia (U+1F7B), are replaced
by the same letter with a \fItonos\fR accent, upsilon with tonos (U+03CD), as Unicode treats oxia and tonos as the same
accent. This affects most polytonic Greek text.
.IP \(bu
The Angstrom sign (U+212B) is replaced by the letter A with a ring above (U+00C5), the Ohm sign (U+2126) by the Greek
capital Omega (U+03A9) and the Kelvin sign (U+212A) by the letter \fCK\fR.
.IP \(bu
The Greek question mark (U+037E) is replaced by a semicolon and the Greek ano teleia (U+0387) by a middle dot (U+00B7).
.IP \(bu
The CJK compatibility ideographs (U+F900 to U+FAFF and U+2F800 to U+2FA1F) are replaced by the matching unified
ideographs, which some fonts draw differently.
.PP
This is what all software which follows the Unicode standard does, so the result matches what other programs produce
when searching or comparing text. It does mean the original codes cannot be got back by running the other command,
e.g. decomposing U+1F7B gives upsilon (U+03C5) followed by a combining acute accent (U+0301), which composes to U+03CD;
use
.Ht undo 2
instead. When the original codes must be kept, do not run either command on that text.
.PP
Neither command applies the compatibility forms (NFKC and NFKD), e.g. a ligature is left as it is. A raw byte (see
.Ht $buffer-encoding 5 )
is left as it is.
.PP
A combining mark is only composed with a letter in the same region, and the region is changed a line at a time. A line
whose text would become longer than the line length limit is not changed and the command fails at it. The change can
be undone with
.Ht undo 2 .
.PP
These commands are only available in a version of \*(mS with Unicode support.
